---

<section id="faq" class="bg-canvas py-20">
	<div class="container-page max-w-2xl">
		<h2 class="text-display-lg mb-12">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

		<div class="space-y-4">
			<!-- FAQ Item 1 -->
			<details class="card-marketing group cursor-pointer">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-2">
					What is a contrast ratio?
					<span class="text-xl group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body mt-4 pb-2">
					Contrast ratio is the difference in luminance (brightness) between two colors. It's measured on
					a scale from 1:1 (no contrast) to 21:1 (maximum contrast). Higher ratios mean better readability.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 2 -->
			<details class="card-marketing group cursor-pointer">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-2">
					Why does contrast matter for accessibility?
					<span class="text-xl group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body mt-4 pb-2">
					Low contrast makes text harder or impossible to read for people with low vision, color
					blindness, and other visual impairments. It also affects all users in bright sunlight or on
					older displays. Sufficient contrast is essential for legal accessibility compliance (WCAG).
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 3 -->
			<details class="card-marketing group cursor-pointer">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-2">
					What's the difference between AA and AAA?
					<span class="text-xl group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body mt-4 pb-2">
					<strong>AA</strong> is the baseline standard required by law in most countries (ADA, EN 301
					549, etc.). <strong>AAA</strong> is enhanced and recommended for sensitive content like medical,
					educational, or critical information. Most websites target AA.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 4 -->
			<details class="card-marketing group cursor-pointer">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-2">
					What counts as "large text"?
					<span class="text-xl group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body mt-4 pb-2">
					Per WCAG 2.2, large text is: 18px (or larger) AND 400 weight, OR 14px (or larger) AND 700
					weight. Large text requires a lower contrast ratio (3:1 AA, 4.5:1 AAA) because size aids
					readability.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 5 -->
			<details class="card-marketing group cursor-pointer">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-2">
					Does color blindness affect contrast requirements?
					<span class="text-xl group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body mt-4 pb-2">
					Contrast ratio is based on luminance, not color. So a red/green pair with sufficient luminance
					contrast will meet WCAG even if a colorblind user can't distinguish the colors. However, you
					should never use color alone to convey information. Always add labels, patterns, or icons.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 6 -->
			<details class="card-marketing group cursor-pointer">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-2">
					How do I test my website's contrast?
					<span class="text-xl group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body mt-4 pb-2">
					Use ContrastLab to test individual color pairs. For whole pages, use browser extensions like
					WAVE, Axe DevTools, or Lighthouse (built into Chrome DevTools). Test in real-world conditions:
					different lighting, older devices, and different browsers.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 7 -->
			<details class="card-marketing group cursor-pointer">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-2">
					Can I export my color checks?
					<span class="text-xl group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body mt-4 pb-2">
					Yes! ContrastLab generates shareable URLs for any color pair. You can also export CSS variables
					and Tailwind classes directly. Save your favorite palettes for reuse. API and bulk export (CSV,
					JSON) are coming soon.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 8 -->
			<details class="card-marketing group cursor-pointer">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-2">
					What about WCAG 3.0 and APCA?
					<span class="text-xl group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body mt-4 pb-2">
					WCAG 3.0 is in development and will use APCA (Advanced Perceptual Contrast Algorithm), which is
					more accurate for modern displays. ContrastLab is built with future standards in mind. WCAG 2.2
					remains the current legal standard.
				</p>
			</details>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<style>
	details summary {
		list-style: none;
	}

	details summary::-webkit-details-marker {
		display: none;
	}
</style>
